Describe orientation angular motion and the acceleration of a rotating point (@physics-rotation-motion: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Balance-angular-momentum-about-a-declared-origin.lingua#r_00092886C87E4AA85B6AAE729A") { r_00A972EA7F9A9AF87E148752A8
A mark on a wheel lets us distinguish orientation from position of its center. Begin with a rigid body turning about a fixed axis in an inertial frame. Rigid means that distances between its material points remain constant in this model; it does not mean that every point has the same velocity. In a plane perpendicular to the axis, choose the positive angular direction counterclockwise as viewed from the positive z direction. An angle θ in radians is arc length divided by radius. One turn is 2π radians. A single orientation determines θ only modulo 2π; a continuous unwrapped angle records how many turns occurred. Samples of orientation without sufficient time information can miss complete turns.

Reuse [Describe planar motion with components and a declared frame](Describe-planar-motion-with-components-and-a-declared-frame.lingua#r_0F215E6253BB0BF99F4B22E201), [Connect turning effects to force geometry and moment origins](Connect-turning-effects-to-force-geometry-and-moment-origins.lingua#r_0EFFA51CCA2E1922AFE1E99879) and [oriented-circle definitions](../../mathematics/core/Define-periodic-functions-from-an-oriented-unit-circle.lingua#r_00ACDC81906359539979A61820), [periodic derivatives](../../mathematics/core/Differentiate-periodic-functions-and-connect-their-accumulated-values.lingua#r_00D9B07DB0E8262348D597FDAC), [sampling investigation](../../mathematics/core/Investigate-periodic-states-exact-rotations-and-sampling-ambiguity.lingua#r_001D348AE25C32431381761409). For a material point a perpendicular distance R from the axis, write r=(R cos θ,R sin θ,0). Define e_r=(cos θ,sin θ,0) and e_t=(−sin θ,cos θ,0). With angular velocity ω=dθ/dt and angular acceleration α=dω/dt, differentiating the sine and cosine components gives de_r/dt=ωe_t and de_t/dt=−ωe_r. Consequently v=Rωe_t and a=Rαe_t−Rω²e_r. The radial term remains when α=0; constant speed around a circle is not zero acceleration. These formulas require constant R and the declared fixed axis.

Angular velocity has units rad/s and angular acceleration rad/s²; radians are dimensionless but retain their explanatory role. Torque, angular momentum and moment of inertia will have different units. If θ(t)=θ₀+ω₀t+αt²/2 for constant α, its derivative is ω₀+αt. The displacement formula needs an initial condition; writing θ=αt²/2 silently assumes both θ₀=0 and ω₀=0. A negative angular velocity is allowed, and positive α can first slow a negative rotation before reversing it.

For an original numerical example take R=0.2 m, ω=3 rad/s and α=−2 rad/s². Tangential speed is 0.6 m/s, tangential acceleration is −0.4 m/s² and inward radial acceleration has magnitude 1.8 m/s². The acceleration magnitude is √3.4 m/s² because the components are perpendicular. After one second under this constant α, ω=1 rad/s and the angular displacement is 2 radians. The inward acceleration has then changed to 0.2 m/s²; constant angular acceleration does not mean constant vector acceleration.

An instantaneous angular-velocity vector in three dimensions points along the rotation axis with the right-hand sign. In fixed Cartesian components its action on a relative position ρ is ω×ρ. The cross product of a and b is (a_y b_z−a_z b_y,a_z b_x−a_x b_z,a_x b_y−a_y b_x); it is bilinear, reverses sign when its arguments swap and vanishes for parallel vectors. For ω=(0,0,ω), this reproduces the planar velocity above. Finite three-dimensional orientations cannot generally be added as vectors: order matters. Full rotation matrices, rotating-frame derivatives and gyroscopic motion remain later formation; [Translating and rotating reference frames](../../engineering/mechanical-engineering-and-machine-design/dynamics/Translating-and-rotating-reference-frames.lingua#r_0J66JQV19DX8MJZ371A3YE45Q6) gives that context without becoming a required advanced exercise here.

**Work:** recover both derivatives from the component equations; explain why two marks at different R share ω but have different speeds. Reconstruct an unwrapped angular history consistent with three supplied orientations and give another history differing by complete turns. State what timing or speed bound would distinguish them. For source study use [University Physics Volume 1 — OpenStax](../fluid-dynamics/sources/University-Physics-Volume-1.lingua#r_2W7QCEHZ45XDB3AWVG9NTV36F6), chapters 10–11 progressively; the source inspection note distinguishes actually inspected sections from this broader assignment.

**Continue:** [Balance angular momentum about a declared origin](Balance-angular-momentum-about-a-declared-origin.lingua#r_00092886C87E4AA85B6AAE729A).

